# the tasklist-first guard's portable satisfier — per-phase working state, not a
# deliverable, and it carries a nonce that is meaningless outside its session
#
# THE LEADING SLASH IS CORRECT AND MUST STAY. "Un-anchoring" this line would be wrong:
# per git's own documentation, a pattern
# containing a slash anywhere other than the trailing position is ALREADY anchored to the
# .gitignore's directory — so the leading `/` is redundant here, and `**/.claude/tasklist.md`
# would be strictly LOOSER, not tighter. The real concern is that this
# path is not the only place a tasklist can live: the writer honours `PLC_TASKLIST_FILE` and will
# put one anywhere. A static pattern cannot follow an override, so the answer is an invariant
# that RESOLVES the configured path — scripts/invariants/tasklist-never-tracked.sh — not a
# looser glob.
/.claude/tasklist.md

# The side-discovery queue — working state, and the one thing
# in a phase that is deliberately NOT durable. Lines move from `untriaged` to a landing while a
# phase runs; what becomes permanent is the phase's OUTCOME, written into the journal at close, and
# phase mode CHECKS that (row 13 reads the journal fragment). This repository's own rule
# is that documents record moments and never states, and a half-drained queue is a state.
#
# ONE FILE PER PHASE, not per directory. This block used to say "per-branch working state", and the
# wording is quoted rather than deleted because it was the bug: nothing read a branch name, the file
# belonged to the directory on disk, and the PREVIOUS phase's drained queue satisfied the next
# phase's close. The path now carries the phase id
# (`.claude/sidelines/<phase>.md`, from the manifest's `{PHASE}` template).
#
# SAME LEADING-SLASH REASONING AS THE TASKLIST ABOVE, and for the same reason it is not repeated as
# a looser glob: the queue path is overridable (`PLC_SIDELINES_FILE`), a static pattern cannot
# follow an override, so the answer is an invariant that RESOLVES the configured path —
# scripts/invariants/sidelines-never-tracked.sh. That script tests TRACKED before IGNORED, because
# an ignore rule has no effect on a path already in the index and reversing the order produces a
# true failure with a false reason.
/.claude/sidelines/
# The previous fixed path. KEPT so a queue left behind by the migration stays ignored rather
# than appearing as an untracked file in someone's status. This line is MIGRATION SCAFFOLDING, not a
# correctness rule, and its removal is filed as a backlog entry with a Trigger and an Exit rather
# than left to a comment nobody re-reads.
/.claude/sidelines.md

# p36: the synthesized decision packets handed to the cross-family blind 2nd agent, and its raw
# JSON replies. A cache by construction — every field they carry is transcribed verbatim into the
# phase's qa-log fragment, which IS the audit trail. Keeping the packets too would be a second
# copy of the same record, and this repository has filed what a second copy does often enough.
/.claude/packets/

# The CI state a producer puts on record for the welcome-back card. MACHINE-LOCAL by
# construction — it says what ONE machine observed about ONE commit at ONE moment, so committing it
# would publish a fact about somebody else's clone. Worse, a checkout of an older branch would then
# hand the card a green recorded against a commit nobody is standing on.
#
# Leading slash for the same reason as the line above — a pattern with an interior slash is already
# anchored, so `/` is the correct spelling and `**/` would be strictly looser. Unlike the tasklist,
# this path has NO environment override to follow: scripts/session_card.py resolves
# `<root>/.claude/ci-status.json` and nothing else. Enforced by
# scripts/invariants/ci-status-never-tracked.sh, which checks TRACKED before IGNORED because git's
# ignore rules do not apply to tracked files.
/.claude/ci-status.json

# the marker vocabulary the ROADMAP progress detectors match against. It is
# per-user ON PURPOSE: for a ROADMAP not written in English, a detector for "this
# paragraph restates progress" needs tokens in that language, and those belong to the user's own
# documents, not to the tracked scripts. Externalising the vocabulary keeps them out of the
# deliverable. The scripts read it when present; PLC_ROADMAP_MARKERS overrides the path.
/.claude/roadmap-progress-markers.txt

# Downloaded model caches from semantic-search tooling (e.g. an ONNX sentence-transformer).
# UNANCHORED on purpose: the directory lands wherever the calling tool's cwd happened to be, not
# at the repo root, so a tool run from a subdirectory leaves it in that subdirectory.
# Tens of MB of binary weights,
# zero relationship to this repo's deliverable, and one `git add -A` away from the history.
**/local_cache/
# machine-local hook state. The fixed hooks write these to ~/.claude/plc-state/<repo>/
# instead, so on a current plugin build these paths are never created — but a session running an
# OLDER cached build still writes them here, and an unignored file dirties the tree, which is one
# of close-gate-nudge.sh's own trigger conditions.
# Kept as a permanent floor rather than removed once everyone upgrades — two
# lines, against a failure that is silent.
#
# UNANCHORED on purpose. These were `/.claude/...`, i.e. repo-root only — but the old
# build writes to `<cwd>/.claude/...`, and `cwd` is wherever the shell happens to be standing, not
# the repo root. A stamp written while a shell sits in a subdirectory lands in that
# subdirectory's `.claude/`, is NOT covered by an anchored rule, and a `git add -A` would
# track it into the product tree — where it ships to every adopter and churns on every run. The
# net has to be as deep as the thing it is catching. `scripts/invariants/no-hook-state-tracked.sh`
# is the backstop for the same class at any path this list has not thought of.
**/.claude/.close-gate-nudge-last
**/.claude/.clear-nudge-last
**/.claude/.gate-runs/

# session-local resume artifact (never publish)
# NB: anchor to root so only the top-level RESUME.md is ignored — an unanchored
# `RESUME.md` matches any nested path of that name on case-insensitive filesystems
# (macOS APFS) and could wrongly ignore a shipped file.
/RESUME.md
# cold sibling written by /handoff (archive-before-overwrite) — holds verbatim
# outgoing snapshots of session state; never publish.
/RESUME-archive.md

# local embedding-model cache (ONNX weights etc.) pulled by dev tooling — a large
# binary blob, never part of the skill deliverable; keep it out of history.
/local_cache/

# transient mutation-run artifact: test-hooks.sh AC5 copies the hook next to the real one to
# prove sibling resolution, and an interrupted run can outlive its cleanup trap. Never committed.
**/context-floor.AC5-mutant.sh

# Codex-host harness artifacts: per-machine instructions and hook wiring for a Codex CLI session,
# not authored here. Ignored rather than
# committed: they are per-machine host wiring, and an untracked file no rule covers is
# one `git add -A` away from entering history unreviewed.
/.codex/
/AGENTS.md
